PIKE

Pike is a general-purpose, interpreted programming language with a
syntax similar to C. It is for programs where you want the
convenience of a scripting language without giving up types or
speed. It is easy to learn, has no separate compile step, and has
powerful built-in data types that make data handling simple and fast.

  o Types as strict as you want them. Declare int, string or
    mapping(string(8bit):array(int(4..72))) and the compiler checks
    them. Use mixed where you'd rather not.

  o Rich built-in data types. Arrays, mappings (hash tables) and
    multisets are part of the language. Strings are Unicode, indexed by
    character. Integers grow into bignums as needed, and memory is
    managed automatically.

  o Programs are values. Classes, functions and closures are
    first-class, and code can be compiled at runtime.

  o A large standard library. HTTP, TLS, DNS, SMTP, LDAP, JSON, XML,
    SQL (MySQL/MariaDB, PostgreSQL, SQLite, ODBC), cryptography
    (Nettle), image processing and more, all maintained together with
    the language.

  o Old code keeps running. A program can declare the version it was
    written for (#pike 8.0), and a compatibility layer keeps it working
    after a major upgrade of the language.

Run pike without arguments for an interactive prompt (Hilfe).

Documentation and a tutorial: https://pike.lysator.liu.se/docs/
New releases: https://pike.lysator.liu.se/download/
Questions and discussion: https://pike.lysator.liu.se/forums/


HOW TO BUILD PIKE

If you are building Pike from git, read README-GIT instead.

The top-level makefile (in this directory, not the src directory) has
all the magic you need to build Pike. Just type 'make'. To install
type "make install" or "make install_interactive".

You will need a C compiler and libgmp. You probably also want to have
GNU make and libnettle.

If that doesn't work, refer to README-GIT, in particular the sections
on additional "DEPENDENCIES", on "CONFIGURE OPTIONS AND BUILD
VARIABLES", on "MANUAL BUILDING" and, if needed, the section "IF IT
DOESN'T WORK".


BUGS

Report bugs at https://git.lysator.liu.se/pikelang/pike/-/issues and
include as much as you can muster of the following:

  o The version of the interpreter. (Try pike --version or look in
    src/version.h)
  o What kind of system hardware/software you use (OS, compiler, etc.)
  o The piece of code that crashes or misbehaves, preferably in a
    very small Pike script with the bug isolated. Please send a
    complete running example that triggers the bug.
  o A description of what goes wrong and when.
  o If you know how, then also give us a backtrace and dump of vital
    variables at the point of crash.
  o Or, if you found the error and corrected it, open a merge request
    at https://git.lysator.liu.se/pikelang/pike with a description
    of what you did and why.

If you find a bug in the interpreter and want to debug it yourself,
first make sure the interpreter is built with runtime debug checks:
configure with --with-rtldebug (or --with-debug, which also adds
debug symbols), rebuild, and see if you get another error.


HISTORY

Pike has a long history in high-traffic network servers. It began in
1994 as µLPC, Fredrik Hübinette's standalone take on LPC, the language
of LPMud game servers. It was renamed Pike in 1996 and grew up as the
language of the Roxen web server. Today it is maintained by the Pike
team and the wider community, and hosted by Lysator at Linköping
University.


